00:00:05.555 --> 00:00:06.930
SPEAKER: With
Cloud Shell Editor,

00:00:06.930 --> 00:00:08.910
you can easily run code
from your web browser

00:00:08.910 --> 00:00:10.290
and deploy it to the cloud.

00:00:10.290 --> 00:00:11.940
In previous videos,
we learned how

00:00:11.940 --> 00:00:14.580
to deploy Hello, World
applications to Cloud Run

00:00:14.580 --> 00:00:16.762
and Google Kubernetes Engine.

00:00:16.762 --> 00:00:18.720
But what if you want to
extend your application

00:00:18.720 --> 00:00:21.270
to use other Google Cloud APIs?

00:00:21.270 --> 00:00:24.900
Cloud Shell Editor's Cloud
Code integration makes it easy.

00:00:24.900 --> 00:00:26.895
Cloud Code is a
set of IDE plugins

00:00:26.895 --> 00:00:28.770
that help you stay
productive when developing

00:00:28.770 --> 00:00:30.870
cloud-native applications.

00:00:30.870 --> 00:00:34.170
In particular, Cloud Code
has a Cloud API section

00:00:34.170 --> 00:00:37.170
that helps you easily browse
available Google Cloud APIs,

00:00:37.170 --> 00:00:41.550
enable them, and add API client
libraries to your project.

00:00:41.550 --> 00:00:44.070
From the Cloud Vision
API to the Cloud Storage

00:00:44.070 --> 00:00:49.020
API to the BigQuery API, the
possibilities are endless.

00:00:49.020 --> 00:00:51.270
Let's take a look at how to
integrate the Google Cloud

00:00:51.270 --> 00:00:54.750
Translate API into a
Python Flask web app.

00:00:54.750 --> 00:00:56.640
First, visit
ide.cloud.google.com

00:00:56.640 --> 00:00:59.790
to launch Cloud Shell Editor.

00:01:02.550 --> 00:01:06.270
Select Terminal, New Terminal to
open a new Cloud Shell terminal

00:01:06.270 --> 00:01:07.750
window.

00:01:07.750 --> 00:01:10.740
From here, run git clone
to clone the sample code

00:01:10.740 --> 00:01:12.450
from GitHub.

00:01:12.450 --> 00:01:14.550
Then we'll open the
sample application

00:01:14.550 --> 00:01:17.610
as a new workspace with the
cloudshell workspace command.

00:01:22.520 --> 00:01:25.670
You can see the list of files
in the Explorer panel located

00:01:25.670 --> 00:01:28.220
on the left side of the screen.

00:01:28.220 --> 00:01:29.990
In the Cloud Shell
terminal window,

00:01:29.990 --> 00:01:32.240
change directories to the
location of the sample app.

00:01:35.330 --> 00:01:37.830
To run the sample app locally,
run the following terminal

00:01:37.830 --> 00:01:38.330
commands.

00:01:41.680 --> 00:01:44.620
Press Command and click on the
URL displayed to open your app.

00:01:48.070 --> 00:01:49.330
Congratulations.

00:01:49.330 --> 00:01:51.700
If you see this page, that
means you've successfully

00:01:51.700 --> 00:01:55.360
run the Hello, World app locally
using Cloud Shell Editor.

00:01:55.360 --> 00:01:57.160
Back in the Cloud
Shell terminal window,

00:01:57.160 --> 00:02:00.340
press Control-C to
stop the local server.

00:02:00.340 --> 00:02:02.170
Now let's set up
credentials so that we

00:02:02.170 --> 00:02:03.730
can authenticate
Google Cloud API

00:02:03.730 --> 00:02:06.070
requests for local developments.

00:02:06.070 --> 00:02:07.870
In the Cloud Shell
terminal window,

00:02:07.870 --> 00:02:11.680
run gcloud config set project
to set the Google Cloud

00:02:11.680 --> 00:02:15.940
project you want to use,
then run gcloud auth

00:02:15.940 --> 00:02:19.480
application-default login.

00:02:19.480 --> 00:02:21.880
When prompted in the terminal
if you want to continue,

00:02:21.880 --> 00:02:24.760
type in Y and press Enter.

00:02:24.760 --> 00:02:27.850
Press Command and click the
link displayed in the terminal.

00:02:27.850 --> 00:02:30.040
This opens a new browser tab.

00:02:30.040 --> 00:02:32.110
Go ahead and authorize
access to your account.

00:02:36.940 --> 00:02:39.400
Copy the authentication
code displayed and paste it

00:02:39.400 --> 00:02:40.210
into your terminal.

00:02:44.960 --> 00:02:47.510
Now let's go ahead and
integrate a new Cloud API

00:02:47.510 --> 00:02:49.370
into our application.

00:02:49.370 --> 00:02:53.970
First, click on the API icon on
the left side of your screen.

00:02:53.970 --> 00:02:55.910
This opens the API
Explorer, where

00:02:55.910 --> 00:02:58.280
you can browse the dozens
of Google Cloud APIs for you

00:02:58.280 --> 00:02:59.900
to use.

00:02:59.900 --> 00:03:01.930
Select the Cloud
Translation API,

00:03:01.930 --> 00:03:03.680
which allows you to
translate between more

00:03:03.680 --> 00:03:05.000
than 100 languages.

00:03:07.650 --> 00:03:09.995
Under Enable API,
click Enable API.

00:03:15.830 --> 00:03:19.310
Under Install Client
Library, select Python.

00:03:19.310 --> 00:03:21.650
Lets copy the name of
the translation library

00:03:21.650 --> 00:03:24.960
and paste it into our
requirements.txt file,

00:03:24.960 --> 00:03:30.050
which lists out Python
libraries we need for our app.

00:03:30.050 --> 00:03:33.260
Now we can configure our Python
environment using virtual env

00:03:33.260 --> 00:03:35.210
and install
dependencies using pip.

00:03:41.090 --> 00:03:42.890
Now that the client
library's installed,

00:03:42.890 --> 00:03:45.350
let's translate some text.

00:03:45.350 --> 00:03:49.430
Open the web.py file to
edit the Python code.

00:03:49.430 --> 00:03:52.590
First, add a line at the top to
import the Translation client

00:03:52.590 --> 00:03:53.090
library.

00:03:57.620 --> 00:04:00.410
Next, we're going to replace
our existing root directory

00:04:00.410 --> 00:04:02.120
code with the following.

00:04:02.120 --> 00:04:05.870
This translates the Hello,
World text into Spanish.

00:04:05.870 --> 00:04:08.900
Finally, we'll run the updated
application with flask run.

00:04:13.650 --> 00:04:14.820
Congratulations.

00:04:14.820 --> 00:04:17.040
If you see this page
with the Spanish text,

00:04:17.040 --> 00:04:19.560
you successfully integrated
the Cloud Translation API

00:04:19.560 --> 00:04:21.168
into your code.

00:04:21.168 --> 00:04:22.710
With just a couple
of clicks, we were

00:04:22.710 --> 00:04:24.630
able to easily integrate
a new Google Cloud

00:04:24.630 --> 00:04:26.770
API into our project.

00:04:26.770 --> 00:04:28.350
Want to try this out yourself?

00:04:28.350 --> 00:04:29.790
Check out the
interactive tutorial

00:04:29.790 --> 00:04:32.040
linked in the description
below, or by launching it

00:04:32.040 --> 00:04:34.200
from the Cloud Shell
Editor welcome page.

